@ 338 T1 literals and comments full of SQL; CTEs named like base tables
value crm.customer.customer_id -> <result>.customer_id identity
value crm.customer.customer_name -> <result>.customer_name identity
value crm.contact.email -> <result>.contact_email identity
value crm.contact.email -> <result>.primary_email identity
value crm.contact.is_primary -> <result>.primary_email conditional
value crm.customer.segment -> <result>.select identity
value crm.customer.status -> <result>.from identity
value crm.customer.country_code -> <result>.we"ird identity
value crm.customer.customer_id, crm.customer.status -> <result>.row_text derived
filter crm.customer.created_at -> <result>
note The four SQL-bearing literals, NULL::text, now() and (SELECT 1) read no column. `cu` is crm.customer (qualified); `k` is the CTE `customer` over crm.contact; `ct` is crm.contact (qualified), so the CTE `contact` is never read. k's join is a key equality (D-5).
forbid value crm.contact.email -> <result>.customer_name | cu is the qualified crm.customer; the CTE named customer is k
forbid value crm.customer.customer_name -> <result>.contact_email | k is the CTE named customer, which reads crm.contact
forbid value evt.flag.flag_code -> <result>.primary_email | ct is the qualified crm.contact; the CTE named contact is never read
forbid filter crm.contact.is_primary -> <result> | a LEFT join's one-relation ON condition removes no row (D-48)

@ 369 T2 ORDER BY an OUTPUT alias, with LIMIT
value crm.customer.segment -> <result>.status identity
value crm.customer.customer_id -> <result>.seg_customer identity
filter crm.customer.segment -> <result>
note PostgreSQL resolves a bare ORDER BY name to an output column first: `status` is c.segment (D-9).
forbid filter crm.customer.status -> <result> | ORDER BY status names the output alias (segment), not the input column

@ 376 T3 GROUP BY a name that is an INPUT column
value crm.customer.segment -> <result>.status aggregated
influence crm.customer.status -> <result>.status, <result>.n
note PostgreSQL resolves a GROUP BY name to an input column first: GROUP BY status is c.status, not MAX(c.segment).
forbid influence crm.customer.segment -> <result>.n | GROUP BY status names the input column c.status, not the output alias

@ 382 T4 INSERT with look-alike literals and comments
value crm.customer.customer_id -> mart.customer_snapshot.customer_id identity
value crm.customer.customer_name -> mart.customer_snapshot.customer_name identity
value crm.customer.segment -> mart.customer_snapshot.segment identity
filter crm.customer.status, crm.customer.customer_name, crm.customer.segment -> mart.customer_snapshot
note status is the literal 'ACTIVE' and updated_at is now(): nothing. The `;` and 'FROM crm.contact' are inside literals.
forbid value crm.customer.credit_limit -> mart.customer_snapshot.segment | only a comment names credit_limit
